;; Docs for std/os.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "os.bjodoc"). The texts are
;; samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "The operating system the program runs on, the machine and the process, the user's standard folders, and signals.")
  (see windows? linux? os-platform? windows-version-at-least? os-description processor-count config-directory
       with-signal-cancel on-signal signal-subscribe)
  (example """
    (import (std os))

    (: num-lock? (-> (Option bool)))
    (defun (num-lock? )
      (cond
        ((windows?) (Some (windows-num-lock?)))   ; from a module of its own
        ((linux?) (linux-num-lock?))
        (else None)))
    """)
  (example """
    (when (windows-version-at-least? 10 #:build 22000)
      (displayln "Windows 11"))
    """)
  (reference """
    A program is built once and runs on every system, so the choice is made
    when it runs. Each check is a call of the .NET method itself. .NET's
    core library is built for one system, so on that system the method
    returns a constant: the JIT removes the branch that cannot run, and the
    check costs nothing.

    Code for another system can be in a module of its own, imported
    everywhere. A module's definitions run the first time something in it
    is used, so on a system where nothing calls it, it does nothing. Every
    module must still compile on the machine that builds it: a .NET method
    that exists only on one system, as @code{Console.NumberLock} on Windows,
    is declared everywhere and throws where it is not supported.

    The facts about the machine and the process are .NET's
    @code{RuntimeInformation} and @code{Environment}. The folders are
    @code{Environment.GetFolderPath}, which follows the XDG variables on
    Linux, and a cache folder, which .NET does not have. A folder is given
    whether or not it exists yet: create it before you write to it.

    @strong{Signals.} A program that does nothing about signals ends at
    once on Ctrl-C or SIGTERM: no @code{finally} runs and no scope releases
    what it owns. @code{with-signal-cancel} is a scope that the first of its
    signals cancels, so the program cleans up and goes on. A second signal
    does the default action and ends the program. @code{on-signal} calls a
    function for each signal that comes, as for SIGHUP to read the
    configuration again. @code{signal-subscribe} and @code{signal-recv} give
    the signals as values to a fiber.
    """)
  (example """
    ;; Where a program keeps its settings.
    (def settings (path-combine (config-directory) "my-app" "settings.bjodat"))
    (directory-create (path-directory settings))
    """)
  (example """
    (defbjo (main args)
      (match (with-signal-cancel [Interrupt Terminate]
               (serve config))
        ((Ok _) 0)
        ((Err s) (displayln (str "stopped by " (signal-name s))) 0)))
    """))

(:doc windows?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on Windows.")
  (returns "#t on Windows.")
  (see windows-version-at-least? os-platform?))

(:doc linux?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on Linux. Not on Android, which has android?.")
  (returns "#t on Linux.")
  (see android? os-platform?))

(:doc macos?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on macOS. Not on Mac Catalyst, which has mac-catalyst?.")
  (returns "#t on macOS.")
  (see macos-version-at-least? mac-catalyst? os-platform?))

(:doc freebsd?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on FreeBSD.")
  (returns "#t on FreeBSD.")
  (see freebsd-version-at-least? os-platform?))

(:doc android?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on Android.")
  (returns "#t on Android.")
  (see android-version-at-least? linux?))

(:doc ios?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on iOS. Also true on Mac Catalyst, which runs iOS programs.")
  (returns "#t on iOS and Mac Catalyst.")
  (see ios-version-at-least? mac-catalyst?))

(:doc mac-catalyst?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on Mac Catalyst, iOS on a Mac.")
  (returns "#t on Mac Catalyst.")
  (see mac-catalyst-version-at-least? ios? macos?))

(:doc tvos?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on tvOS.")
  (returns "#t on tvOS.")
  (see tvos-version-at-least?))

(:doc watchos?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on watchOS.")
  (returns "#t on watchOS.")
  (see watchos-version-at-least?))

(:doc browser?
  (summary "Whether the program runs in a browser, as WebAssembly.")
  (returns "#t in a browser.")
  (see wasi?))

(:doc wasi?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on WASI, WebAssembly outside a browser.")
  (returns "#t on WASI.")
  (see browser?))

(:doc os-platform?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on the system of this name.")
  (arg platform "The name, in any case: \"Windows\", \"Linux\", \"macOS\" or \"OSX\", \"FreeBSD\", \"Android\", \"iOS\", \"MacCatalyst\", \"tvOS\", \"watchOS\", \"Browser\" or \"WASI\".")
  (returns "#t on that system. A name that is no system is #f.")
  (see os-platform-version-at-least? windows? linux?)
  (example """
    (os-platform? "linux")   ; #t on Linux
    """)
  (reference """
    As the checks of their own, @code{"iOS"} is also true on Mac Catalyst.
    """))

(:doc windows-version-at-least?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on Windows of this version or later.")
  (arg major "The major version.")
  (key minor "The minor version. 0 unless given.")
  (key build "The build number. 0 unless given.")
  (key revision "The revision. 0 unless given.")
  (returns "#t on Windows of that version or later; #f on any other system.")
  (see windows?)
  (example """
    (windows-version-at-least? 10 #:build 22000)   ; Windows 11 or later
    """))

(:doc macos-version-at-least?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on macOS of this version or later.")
  (arg major "The major version.")
  (key minor "The minor version. 0 unless given.")
  (key build "The build number. 0 unless given.")
  (returns "#t on macOS of that version or later; #f on any other system.")
  (see macos?)
  (example """
    (macos-version-at-least? 14)   ; Sonoma or later
    """))

(:doc freebsd-version-at-least?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on FreeBSD of this version or later.")
  (arg major "The major version.")
  (key minor "The minor version. 0 unless given.")
  (key build "The build number. 0 unless given.")
  (key revision "The revision. 0 unless given.")
  (returns "#t on FreeBSD of that version or later; #f on any other system.")
  (see freebsd?))

(:doc android-version-at-least?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on Android of this version or later.")
  (arg major "The major version.")
  (key minor "The minor version. 0 unless given.")
  (key build "The build number. 0 unless given.")
  (key revision "The revision. 0 unless given.")
  (returns "#t on Android of that version or later; #f on any other system.")
  (see android?))

(:doc ios-version-at-least?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on iOS, or Mac Catalyst, of this version or later.")
  (arg major "The major version.")
  (key minor "The minor version. 0 unless given.")
  (key build "The build number. 0 unless given.")
  (returns "#t on iOS or Mac Catalyst of that version or later; #f on any other system.")
  (see ios?))

(:doc mac-catalyst-version-at-least?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on Mac Catalyst of this version or later.")
  (arg major "The major version.")
  (key minor "The minor version. 0 unless given.")
  (key build "The build number. 0 unless given.")
  (returns "#t on Mac Catalyst of that version or later; #f on any other system.")
  (see mac-catalyst?))

(:doc tvos-version-at-least?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on tvOS of this version or later.")
  (arg major "The major version.")
  (key minor "The minor version. 0 unless given.")
  (key build "The build number. 0 unless given.")
  (returns "#t on tvOS of that version or later; #f on any other system.")
  (see tvos?))

(:doc watchos-version-at-least?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on watchOS of this version or later.")
  (arg major "The major version.")
  (key minor "The minor version. 0 unless given.")
  (key build "The build number. 0 unless given.")
  (returns "#t on watchOS of that version or later; #f on any other system.")
  (see watchos?))

(:doc os-platform-version-at-least?
  (summary "Whether the program runs on the system of this name, of this version or later.")
  (arg platform "The name, as os-platform? takes it.")
  (arg major "The major version.")
  (key minor "The minor version. 0 unless given.")
  (key build "The build number. 0 unless given.")
  (key revision "The revision. 0 unless given.")
  (returns "#t on that system, of that version or later.")
  (see os-platform?)
  (reference """
    The version is the one .NET reads for the system. On Linux it is the
    kernel's, as 6.18.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The machine and the process
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc os-description
  (summary "The operating system, as a person reads it.")
  (returns "A string, as \"Fedora Linux 43\" or \"Microsoft Windows 10.0.22631\".")
  (see os-version os-architecture)
  (reference """
    .NET's @code{RuntimeInformation.OSDescription}. Its form differs from
    system to system, so it is for a report or a log, not to be read by a
    program: on Linux it is the distribution's name, from
    @code{/etc/os-release}.
    """))

(:doc os-version
  (summary "The version of the operating system, as .NET reads it.")
  (returns "The parts of the version, most significant first, as [10 0 22631 0] on Windows.")
  (see os-description windows-version-at-least? os-platform-version-at-least?)
  (example """
    (os-version)   ; [6 18 13 200] on Linux 6.18.13-200
    """)
  (reference """
    On Linux it is the kernel's version. A part that .NET does not know is
    left out, so there are two to four parts. To compare it with a version,
    use the version checks, as @code{windows-version-at-least?}.
    """))

(:doc os-architecture
  (summary "The processor architecture of the operating system.")
  (returns "Its name in lower case: \"x64\", \"arm64\", \"x86\", \"arm\", \"armv6\", \"wasm\", \"s390x\", \"loongarch64\", \"ppc64le\" or \"riscv64\".")
  (see process-architecture runtime-identifier)
  (reference """
    The names are .NET's, as a runtime identifier such as
    @code{linux-arm64} writes them. A 32-bit process on a 64-bit system
    has another @code{process-architecture}.
    """))

(:doc process-architecture
  (summary "The processor architecture this process runs as.")
  (returns "Its name in lower case, as os-architecture gives it.")
  (see os-architecture))

(:doc runtime-identifier
  (summary "The runtime identifier of .NET in this process, as \"linux-x64\" or \"win-arm64\".")
  (returns "A string.")
  (see os-architecture dotnet-description)
  (reference """
    A .NET built by a Linux distribution can give one of its own, as
    @code{fedora.43-x64}.
    """))

(:doc dotnet-description
  (summary "The .NET that runs the program, as \".NET 10.0.4\".")
  (returns "A string, for a report or a log.")
  (see runtime-identifier))

(:doc processor-count
  (summary "How many processors this process may use.")
  (returns "At least 1.")
  (see available-memory)
  (reference """
    .NET's @code{Environment.ProcessorCount}: the processors of the
    machine, less what a container's CPU limit or the process's affinity
    leaves out.
    """))

(:doc available-memory
  (summary "How much memory this process may use, in bytes.")
  (returns "A long.")
  (see processor-count)
  (reference """
    The limit the GC works to: the machine's memory, or a smaller limit set
    for a container or for the process. It is not the memory that is free
    at the moment.
    """))

(:doc machine-name
  (summary "The name of this computer on the network.")
  (returns "A string.")
  (see user-name))

(:doc user-name
  (summary "The name of the user the process runs as.")
  (returns "A string.")
  (see machine-name privileged? home-directory))

(:doc process-id
  (summary "This process's id, as the operating system gives it.")
  (returns "An int."))

(:doc process-path
  (summary "The executable that runs this process.")
  (returns "(Some path), or None when the system does not tell.")
  (see process-id)
  (reference """
    A program run by @code{dotnet} is run by the @code{dotnet} host, so
    this is the host's path, not the program's.
    """))

(:doc privileged?
  (summary "Whether the process runs with the rights of an administrator.")
  (returns "#t as root on Unix, and when elevated on Windows.")
  (see user-name))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The user's folders
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc home-directory
  (summary "The user's home directory.")
  (returns "Its path: HOME on Unix, the user's profile on Windows.")
  (see config-directory data-directory cache-directory)
  (reference """
    Raises when the system has none, as when HOME is not set and the user
    has no home in the password database. The other folders raise in the
    same way.
    """))

(:doc config-directory
  (summary "The folder for a program's configuration, which is the user's own.")
  (returns "Its path. Put a program's files in a folder of its own in it.")
  (see data-directory cache-directory home-directory)
  (reference """
    @code{XDG_CONFIG_HOME} on Linux when it is set to an absolute path,
    else @code{~/.config}; the roaming application data, @code{%APPDATA%},
    on Windows; @code{~/Library/Application Support} on macOS, the same
    folder as @code{data-directory}'s. It may not exist yet.
    """))

(:doc data-directory
  (summary "The folder for the data a program keeps for the user on this machine.")
  (returns "Its path. Put a program's files in a folder of its own in it.")
  (see config-directory cache-directory)
  (reference """
    @code{XDG_DATA_HOME} on Linux when it is set to an absolute path, else
    @code{~/.local/share}; the local application data,
    @code{%LOCALAPPDATA%}, on Windows; @code{~/Library/Application Support}
    on macOS. It may not exist yet.
    """))

(:doc cache-directory
  (summary "The folder for files a program can make again, which may be deleted.")
  (returns "Its path. Put a program's files in a folder of its own in it.")
  (see data-directory temp-directory)
  (reference """
    @code{XDG_CACHE_HOME} on Linux and the other Unix systems when it is set
    to an absolute path, else @code{~/.cache}; the local application data
    on Windows; @code{~/Library/Caches} on macOS. .NET has no such folder,
    so this follows the XDG Base Directory specification and the systems'
    own conventions. It may not exist yet.
    """))

(:doc documents-directory
  (summary "The user's folder for documents.")
  (returns "Its path: on Linux the XDG documents folder, as ~/Documents.")
  (see desktop-directory home-directory))

(:doc desktop-directory
  (summary "The user's desktop folder.")
  (returns "Its path: on Linux the XDG desktop folder, as ~/Desktop.")
  (see documents-directory home-directory))

(:doc temp-directory
  (summary "The folder for temporary files.")
  (returns "Its path, without a separator at the end.")
  (see make-temp-directory cache-directory)
  (reference """
    @code{TMPDIR} on Unix when it is set, else @code{/tmp}; @code{TMP} or
    @code{TEMP} on Windows. Other users can write to it too: for files of
    your own, make a directory with @code{make-temp-directory}.
    """))

(:doc make-temp-directory
  (summary "A new, empty directory in the temp folder.")
  (key prefix "The start of its name. Empty unless given.")
  (returns "Its path. Its name is prefix and characters of its own.")
  (see temp-directory directory-delete-tree)
  (example """
    (def work (make-temp-directory #:prefix "my-app-"))   ; "/tmp/my-app-x3kQ9z"
    ;; ...
    (directory-delete-tree work)
    """)
  (reference """
    On Unix only its owner may read, write or enter it. It stays until it
    is deleted.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Signals
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Signal
  (summary "A POSIX signal: a message from the system or from another process to this process.")
  (case Hangup "SIGHUP: the terminal closed. Many daemons read their configuration again on it. On Windows, the console closed.")
  (case Interrupt "SIGINT: Ctrl-C in a terminal, also on Windows.")
  (case Quit "SIGQUIT: Ctrl-\\ in a terminal. On Windows, Ctrl-Break.")
  (case Terminate "SIGTERM: a request to stop, as from kill or systemd. On Windows, log off or shut down.")
  (case Child "SIGCHLD: a child process stopped or ended.")
  (case Continue "SIGCONT: the process continues after a stop.")
  (case WindowChanged "SIGWINCH: the terminal changed its size.")
  (case TerminalInput "SIGTTIN: a background process read from the terminal.")
  (case TerminalOutput "SIGTTOU: a background process wrote to the terminal.")
  (case TerminalStop "SIGTSTP: Ctrl-Z in a terminal.")
  (case User1 "SIGUSR1, for the program's own use: 10 on Linux, 30 on macOS and FreeBSD.")
  (case User2 "SIGUSR2, for the program's own use: 12 on Linux, 31 on macOS and FreeBSD.")
  (case OtherSignal "A signal by its number on this system, as (OtherSignal 34) for SIGRTMIN on Linux.")
  (see signal-subscribe with-signal-cancel signal-name)
  (reference """
    Windows has only @code{Hangup}, @code{Interrupt}, @code{Quit} and
    @code{Terminate}, and a subscription to another signal raises there.
    Microsoft's documentation says that Windows ends the process soon
    after @code{Hangup} and @code{Terminate}, whatever the program does.
    """))

(:doc Signals
  (summary "A subscription to signals, from signal-subscribe.")
  (see signal-subscribe signal-recv signal-unsubscribe!))

(:doc signal-subscribe
  (summary "Subscribes to signals, so that they come as values from signal-recv.")
  (arg signals "A Vec of the signals. A signal that is given twice counts once.")
  (returns "The subscription. The ambient scope owns it.")
  (raises System.PlatformNotSupportedException "When this system does not have one of the signals, or cannot handle it, as SIGKILL.")
  (raises System.ArgumentException "For (OtherSignal n) with n below 1.")
  (raises Bjolang.Runtime.ScopeEnded "When there is no ambient scope, as in the initialiser of a module-level value.")
  (see signal-recv signal-unsubscribe! with-signal-cancel)
  (example """
    (with-scope
      (def signals (signal-subscribe [Hangup Terminate]))
      (let loop ()
        (match (sync (signal-recv signals))
          (Hangup (read-configuration!) (loop))
          (_ unit))))
    """)
  (reference """
    While the subscription lives, its signals do not do their default
    action: SIGTERM does not end the process, and Ctrl-Z does not stop it.
    The default action of @code{Child}, @code{Continue} and
    @code{WindowChanged} is to do nothing, and .NET's own handling of them
    stays. The subscription ends with its scope, or at
    @code{signal-unsubscribe!}, and then the default action is back. When
    it raises, it has subscribed to nothing.

    .NET calls the handler on a thread of its own. The handler puts the
    signal in an inbox with room for one, and each kind of signal has its
    own inbox. So signals of one kind that come before the program
    receives them are merged into one, as in OCaml and Racket. Signals of
    two kinds are two. Every subscription to a signal receives it.

    A signal that is ignored when the program starts stays ignored, and
    does not come. A shell starts a background job with SIGINT and SIGQUIT
    ignored.
    """))

(:doc signal-recv
  (summary "Returns the CML event of the next signal of a subscription.")
  (arg s "The subscription.")
  (returns "An event that yields the signal.")
  (see signal-subscribe with-signal-cancel)
  (reference """
    In a @code{choose} that another branch wins, it takes nothing. When
    signals of more than one kind wait, which one comes first is not given.
    After the subscription ends, it never fires.
    """))

(:doc signal-unsubscribe!
  (summary "Ends a subscription before its scope ends.")
  (arg s "The subscription.")
  (see signal-subscribe)
  (reference """
    The signals do their default action again, and the signals that wait
    are dropped. A second call does nothing.
    """))

(:doc on-signal
  (summary "Calls a function for each of the signals that comes, for as long as the scope lives.")
  (arg signals "A Vec of the signals.")
  (arg handler "What to call with the signal: an ordinary function or a bjoroutine.")
  (raises System.PlatformNotSupportedException "As signal-subscribe.")
  (see signal-subscribe with-signal-cancel in-scope)
  (example """
    (defun (main)
      (on-signal [Interrupt] (fun (s) (println "Ctrl-C: carrying on")))
      (do-the-work)
      0)
    """)
  (example """
    ;; From code deeper down, for the whole program.
    (def program (current-scope))      ; in main
    ...
    (in-scope program
      (on-signal [Hangup] (fun (s) (read-configuration!))))
    """)
  (reference """
    A daemon of the ambient scope calls the handler. So the handler has the
    parameters and effect handlers of the call to @code{on-signal}, and it
    runs at the same time as the code that called it: share state with it
    through @code{(std atomic)} or a channel. Only one call runs at a
    time. A signal that comes during a call waits, merged with the others
    of its kind.

    The signals do not do their default action while the handler is there:
    Ctrl-C does not end the program. When the scope ends, the handler and
    the subscription end together, and the default action is back. In
    @code{main} that is the end of the program. To handle a signal for the
    whole program from code deeper down, call it with @code{in-scope} and
    the scope of @code{main}.

    When the handler raises, the signals do their default action again,
    and the failure is written to standard error as an unhandled exception
    of the daemon. Nothing else would receive the signals.
    """))

(:doc signal-name
  (summary "The POSIX name of a signal.")
  (arg s "The signal.")
  (returns "As \"SIGINT\" for Interrupt, and \"signal 34\" for (OtherSignal 34).")
  (see Signal))

(:doc with-signal-cancel
  (summary "Runs body in a scope that the first of the signals cancels.")
  (form (with-signal-cancel signals body ...))
  (arg signals "A Vec of the signals, as [Interrupt Terminate].")
  (rest body "What to run. The form returns (Ok v) when the body returns v, and (Err s) when the signal s stopped the body.")
  (see signal-subscribe with-cancel with-deadline Signal)
  (example """
    (defbjo (main args)
      (match (with-signal-cancel [Interrupt Terminate]
               (serve config))
        ((Ok _) 0)
        ((Err Interrupt) 130)
        ((Err _) 143)))
    """)
  (reference """
    Without it, Ctrl-C and SIGTERM end the program at once. With it, the
    first signal cancels the scope with @code{(Requested "SIGINT")} and the
    like: every @code{sync} in the scope that has to wait raises
    @code{Cancelled}, the scope waits for its fibers, and it releases what
    it owns. Then the form returns @code{(Err s)}.

    At the first signal the form gives the signals back to the system. So
    a second signal does the default action, and ends the program at once,
    also while it cleans up.

    As with @code{with-deadline}, a body that returns has its value, also
    when the signal stopped its fibers. A failure of the body or of a fiber
    is raised, also after a signal. A cancellation from an outer scope is
    raised too. A signal that this system does not have raises as in
    @code{signal-subscribe}, before the body runs.
    """))

(:doc signal-cancel-arm!
  (summary "Subscribes a scope to signals, so that the first one cancels it. with-signal-cancel writes calls to it.")
  (arg signals "The signals.")
  (arg cancel "The cancel thunk of the scope.")
  (see with-signal-cancel)
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{with-signal-cancel} names
    it.
    """))

(:doc signal-cancel-result
  (summary "The value of with-signal-cancel, from the outcome of its scope. with-signal-cancel writes calls to it.")
  (arg signals "The signals.")
  (arg outcome "The value of the body, or the exception that it left with.")
  (arg token "The token of the scope.")
  (returns "(Ok v) for a value, and (Err s) when the signal s cancelled the scope and the body left because of it.")
  (raises System.Exception "The exception of the body, in every other case.")
  (see with-signal-cancel)
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{with-signal-cancel} names
    it.
    """))
